Sauce Demo – Page Object Model Test Suite
A modular, end-to-end Selenium test suite built with the Page Object Model (POM) design pattern, validating the complete purchase journey on SauceDemo — from login through to order confirmation.

-----------------------
Project Overview
-----------------------
This project refactors a flat, single-class test script into a clean, maintainable POM architecture. Each page of the application has its own dedicated class that owns its locators and interactions. Test classes contain only test logic — no element selectors, no driver setup, no browser configuration. The result is a codebase where a locator change is a one-line fix, and adding a new test takes minutes.

-----------------------
Test Scenarios Covered
-----------------------

User Login – Submits valid credentials and confirms the Products page loads
Add Item to Cart – Adds the Sauce Labs Backpack and verifies the cart badge updates
Navigate to Cart – Opens the cart page and confirms it loads correctly
Initiate Checkout – Proceeds from the cart and verifies the information from the form appears
Enter Customer Information – Fills in shipping details and confirms navigation to the order overview
Complete Order – Clicks Finish and asserts the confirmation message is displayed

------------------
Architecture
------------------
src/
├── test/java/
│   ├── base/
│   │   ├── BasePage.java          # Shared WebDriver reference + PageFactory init
│   │   └── BaseTest.java          # @BeforeClass setup and @AfterClass teardown
│   ├── pages/
│   │   ├── LoginPage.java         # Login form interactions
│   │   ├── InventoryPage.java     # Product catalogue + add to cart
│   │   ├── CartPage.java          # Cart navigation + checkout trigger
│   │   ├── CheckoutInfoPage.java  # Shipping information form
│   │   ├── CheckoutOverviewPage.java  # Order summary + finish
│   │   └── ConfirmationPage.java  # Order confirmation message
│   └── tests/
│       └── OnlineOrderTest.java   # 6 priority-ordered @Test methods
└── pom.xml

----------------------
Concepts Demonstrated
-----------------------

Page Object Model (POM) — Each application page has a dedicated class encapsulating its locators and actions, completely decoupled from test logic
Inheritance — BasePage and BaseTest eliminate duplicated setup code across all page and test classes
PageFactory & @FindBy — Annotation-driven element declaration replaces verbose driver.findElement(By...) calls
Separation of Concerns — Test classes read like plain English; page classes own all UI knowledge
Single Responsibility — Each class has one job; a locator change is a one-file fix
Implicit Waits — Global timeout strategy handles dynamic page loading
TestNG Prioritisation — @Test(priority = n) enforces the correct sequential flow across a stateful journey
Javadoc & Inline Comments — Every class and method is documented for readability and portfolio clarity

----------------
How to Run
----------------
Clone the repository
git clone 
# Run all tests
mvn test
